\section{Introduction}

Autonomous navigation of marine vessels has witnessed significant progress in recent years, driven by advances in control theory, sensor fusion, and data-driven decision-making~\cite{lv2025unmanned, qiao2023survey, alamoush2025maritime}. This is reflected in the growing deployment of unmanned surface vehicles (USVs) for applications ranging from ocean surveying and environmental monitoring to maritime search and rescue and autonomous cargo transport. Despite these developments, achieving robust autonomy in open and confined waterways remains a challenging problem, particularly in the presence of environmental disturbances such as wind, waves, and ocean currents.

A foundational requirement for any autonomous marine system is an accurate kinematic model of the vessel, as high-level planning, motion prediction, and feedback control all depend critically on the fidelity of this underlying representation. Marine vessels are commonly described by a variety of motion models, including the Unicycle model~\cite{uni}, the Bicycle Kinematic model~\cite{bi}, the Three-DOF Ship model~\cite{3dof}, and the Nomoto model~\cite{fossen}, each capturing different aspects of the vessel's translational and rotational dynamics. The choice of motion model therefore directly influences the accuracy of trajectory prediction and the stability of control actions. In practice, however, the true governing model of a deployed vessel is rarely known \textit{a priori}, as it depends on hull geometry, propulsion configuration, loading conditions, and hydrodynamic parameters that are difficult to measure directly~\cite{chen2022grey}. This creates a fundamental model uncertainty problem: autonomous systems must operate under assumptions about vessel dynamics that may not hold across different vessels or operating conditions.

Addressing this uncertainty calls for a principled identification strategy. While full dynamic models that account for hydrodynamic forces, added mass, and damping offer greater physical fidelity, their identification requires force and moment measurements that are impractical to obtain during normal vessel operation~\cite{fossen2011handbook}. Kinematic models, by contrast, describe motion purely in terms of observable pose and velocity quantities, making them directly identifiable from trajectory observations alone --- without access to force inputs or internal vessel states. This motivates the adoption of kinematic representations as the candidate model set in this work, where classification is performed exclusively from the pose trajectory of the ego ship.

Existing approaches to motion model identification typically rely on dedicated sensing modalities, white-box system identification procedures, or explicit access to control inputs and onboard inertial measurements~\cite{herrero2012two, liu2025research}. Physics-based identification methods require extensive hydrodynamic testing and model-specific parameter fitting, which limits their generalizability across vessel types~\cite{araki2012estimating}. Crucially, the majority of these methods treat motion model identification as a regression or parameter estimation problem~\cite{yoon2003identification, herrero2012two}, rather than as a classification task that can be solved directly from observed trajectory data alone~\cite{sebastian2019support}.

In this work, we propose a data-driven framework for ego ship motion model classification that operates exclusively on the observed pose trajectory of the vessel, without requiring access to explicit control inputs, onboard sensor measurements, or model-specific parameter knowledge. From the raw pose sequence, a set of dynamics-oriented kinematic features is derived --- including surge and sway velocities, yaw rate, yaw acceleration, and path curvature --- which encode the intrinsic dynamic characteristics of each motion model while remaining invariant to global coordinate frames and operator-specific maneuver styles. These features are processed by a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) encoder~\cite{bai2018empirical} trained under a joint objective combining supervised contrastive loss~\cite{khosla2020supervised} and cross-entropy loss. The contrastive component enforces intra-class compactness and inter-class separation in the embedding space, while the cross-entropy component directly optimizes class discrimination, enabling the model to generalize across diverse maneuver profiles rather than overfitting to trajectory-specific artifacts. The proposed method avoids dependence on image-based or vision-derived inputs, which are susceptible to degradation under adverse maritime conditions such as fog, glare, and low visibility~\cite{prasad2018object}. Instead, classification is performed entirely from the temporal structure of trajectory-derived kinematic features, making the approach applicable across a broad range of operational environments and vessel configurations. A confidence-weighted aggregation scheme further improves trajectory-level prediction robustness by down-weighting ambiguous temporal windows and amplifying high-confidence predictions. The method is validated across four candidate motion models under diverse simulated maneuver conditions.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work on motion model identification, ship dynamics, and trajectory-based classification. Section~\ref{sec:method} presents the proposed motion model classification framework, encompassing the problem formulation, dynamics-oriented feature extraction pipeline, TCN encoder architecture, joint training objective, and confidence-weighted trajectory-level inference scheme. Section~\ref{sec:results} reports experimental results and comparative analysis. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes with directions for future work.


% =============================================================
\section{Literature Review}
\label{sec:related}
% =============================================================

Accurate kinematic and dynamic modeling of marine vessels has been a central concern in naval architecture and marine control systems for several decades. The Nomoto model, introduced in its first- and second-order forms, remains one of the most widely adopted representations for describing the yaw dynamics of surface ships under helm input~\cite{nomoto}. More comprehensive representations, including the three-degree-of-freedom maneuvering models formulated by Fossen, account for coupled surge, sway, and yaw dynamics and have become standard references in marine control literature~\cite{fossen}. Bicycle kinematic and unicycle models, originally developed for wheeled mobile robots, have also found application in USV guidance owing to their computational simplicity and suitability for low-speed maneuvering scenarios~\cite{structural,dubin}. Despite the availability of these well-established model families, determining which model best governs a specific deployed vessel remains a largely unresolved problem in practice.

Classical system identification approaches address this through dedicated sea trials and regression-based parameter fitting to hydrodynamic databases~\cite{yoon2003identification, alexandersson2022system}. More recent efforts have explored recursive least squares, extended Kalman filter formulations, and grey-box methods that blend physical structure with data-driven parameter learning~\cite{chen2022grey, herrero2012two}. While effective within their respective settings, these approaches share two fundamental limitations. First, they presuppose knowledge of the governing model structure and require either explicit control inputs or dedicated onboard sensing hardware --- restricting applicability to scenarios where the underlying model class is itself unknown. Second, physics-based identification relies on captive model tests involving towing tanks, rotating arms, and planar motion mechanisms, tying identified parameters to a specific vessel's geometry and actuator configuration and fundamentally limiting cross-vessel generalizability~\cite{chen2022grey}. The present work departs from this paradigm entirely by adopting a kinematic perspective, where classification is performed exclusively from observable pose and velocity quantities derived from the trajectory, without requiring any dynamic state or force measurement.

The emergence of machine learning has opened alternative avenues for identifying dynamical systems directly from trajectory data. Sparse identification of nonlinear dynamics (SINDy)~\cite{brunton2016discovering} demonstrated that governing equations can be recovered from trajectory observations via sparse regression over candidate function libraries, with recent extensions such as iNeural-SINDy further improving robustness to noisy and scarce measurements through neural integral-form constraints~\cite{forootani2025robust}. However, such symbolic approaches fundamentally target equation discovery rather than discrimination among a discrete set of candidate model classes from pose observations alone --- a distinct problem that remains unaddressed.

Classification of dynamical systems as a supervised learning problem has received growing attention in robotics and autonomous systems. Prior work has shown that motion response signatures embedded in state trajectory data encode sufficient discriminative information to identify system behavior without dedicated sensing modalities~\cite{sebastian2019support, sermanet2018time}. Analogous ideas have been explored in fault detection and structural health monitoring, where convolutional and recurrent architectures have been applied to classify dynamical system behavior directly from time-series measurements~\cite{xu2025research, zhao2017convolutional}. These works collectively support the hypothesis that trajectory observations alone can discriminate among distinct model classes, provided the feature representation is carefully designed to capture intrinsic dynamic signatures rather than superficial path geometry.

In the maritime domain, Automatic Identification System (AIS) data has been widely used for vessel classification. Beyond kinematic fields such as position, speed over ground, course, and heading, AIS encompasses static vessel attributes --- including ship dimensions, draft, vessel type codes, and MMSI identifiers --- as well as voyage-related information. This breadth of information has enabled classification frameworks that fuse trajectory-derived behavioral features with static geometric metadata to distinguish vessel categories, using classifiers ranging from logistic regression and naive Bayes to ensemble methods and support vector machines~\cite{sheng2018research, zhou2019ship, huang2023ship}. While these works confirm that kinematic signatures embedded in vessel movement data carry discriminative value, they differ fundamentally from the present work in two respects. First, they rely on aggregated statistical features extracted from long-horizon AIS records combined with static vessel metadata, rather than operating on short-horizon pose sequences derived purely from position and heading observations. Second, and more critically, their classification objective is vessel type or operational behavior --- not the underlying kinematic motion model governing the vessel's dynamics. Identifying whether a vessel is a fishing boat or a cargo ship is an entirely different problem from identifying whether its motion is governed by a Nomoto, Unicycle, Three-DOF, or Bicycle Kinematic model. No prior work in the maritime domain has formulated the latter as a data-driven classification problem solvable from pose observations alone.

For sequential and time-series data, recurrent architectures such as Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks have been widely adopted owing to their capacity to model long-range temporal dependencies~\cite{lstm}. However, LSTMs suffer from vanishing gradients over long sequences and are inherently sequential in computation, which limits training efficiency. Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCNs), introduced by Bai et al., address these shortcomings through dilated causal convolutions with residual connections, achieving large receptive fields while remaining fully parallelizable~\cite{bai2018empirical}. TCNs have demonstrated competitive or superior performance relative to recurrent architectures across sequence modeling benchmarks, and have been applied to trajectory prediction~\cite{nikhil2018convolutional} and motion classification tasks~\cite{lea2017temporal}. Extensions combining TCN with Transformer attention mechanisms have further demonstrated advantages over recurrent architectures for early and accurate time-series classification~\cite{chen2022early}, reinforcing the suitability of convolutional temporal encoders for sequential classification tasks. Their ability to capture multi-scale temporal structure through exponentially increasing dilation factors makes TCNs particularly well-suited for encoding the dynamic signatures of vessel maneuvers across different time horizons.

Beyond supervised classification, contrastive learning has emerged as a powerful paradigm for learning structured embedding spaces that generalize across distributional variations. Self-supervised frameworks such as SimCLR and MoCo learn representations by contrasting augmented views of the same sample~\cite{he2020momentum, chen2020simple}, while supervised contrastive learning, proposed by Khosla et al., extends this to the fully labeled setting by treating all same-class samples as positives, enforcing simultaneous intra-class compactness and inter-class separation~\cite{khosla2020supervised}. This approach produces more robust and transferable representations compared to cross-entropy training alone, particularly under distribution shift conditions. In the time-series domain, contrastive objectives have been applied to learn invariant representations across sensor modalities~\cite{eldele2021time}, demonstrating robustness to input variation that is directly relevant to the maneuver diversity encountered in vessel trajectory data.

Taken together, the reviewed literature reveals a clear and unaddressed gap at the intersection of marine motion modeling, trajectory-based classification, and deep sequence learning. Existing ship identification methods operate predominantly within a dynamic modeling paradigm, requiring force measurements, hydrodynamic coefficients, explicit control inputs, or dedicated onboard sensing --- none of which are available when only pose observations of the vessel exist. AIS-based maritime classification methods rely on multi-modal data streams combining static vessel metadata with long-horizon behavioral aggregates, and address vessel type or operational behavior rather than kinematic model identity. Symbolic data-driven approaches such as SINDy target equation discovery rather than discrete model classification. General dynamical system classification methods in robotics have not been extended to the problem of discriminating among candidate kinematic models of marine vessels from short-horizon pose-only trajectory observations. Furthermore, while TCN architectures and supervised contrastive learning have individually demonstrated strong performance in sequence modeling and representation learning, their combination for kinematic motion model classification in the maritime domain has not been previously explored. This work directly addresses all of these gaps by proposing a unified framework that derives dynamics-oriented kinematic features from raw pose trajectories and trains a TCN encoder under a joint contrastive and cross-entropy objective to classify the ego ship's motion model without access to control inputs, onboard sensors, dynamic force measurements, or prior model structure assumptions.
